<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ain Sakhri figurine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ain Sakhri Lovers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ain Sakhri figurine, or Ain Sakhri Lovers, is a Natufian stone sculpture of a couple, about 11,000 years old, found in one of the Ain Sakhri caves near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>. Carved from a single calcite cobble and without faces or other details, it is thought to be the oldest known representation of two people in sexual intercourse. The prehistorian René Neuville identified it in 1933 among finds kept by the French Fathers at Bethlehem and traced it to the caves, which had been lived in during the Natufian period. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> bought it in 1958.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ain-sakhri-figurine.jpg" alt="Ain Sakhri figurine" width="330" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joyofmuseums · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ain_Sakhri_Lovers_-_British_Museum_-_Joy_of_Museums.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
